comments.body:
Maintainer pass: this one stays blocked; I made no changes to the branch.

The branch (337ee655) still does what the earlier reviews asked not to do. `crates/gpu/Cargo.toml` builds only wgpu's `vulkan` feature, `select::backends()` returns Vulkan only and refuses any other `WGPU_BACKEND`, and `apps/fieldcraft/src/main.rs` forces that set on the window. As a result:

- the macOS app has no Metal backend and no bundled MoltenVK, so it cannot open on a stock Mac (the PR description says so too);
- Windows loses DX12 and WARP, and Linux loses the GL fallback;
- a machine with no Vulkan adapter still exits with the error on stderr only, which looks like nothing happening when the app is launched from Finder or Explorer.

Narrowing it to "Vulkan only on Linux and Windows, Metal on macOS" would still drop the DX12 and GL fallbacks and keep the silent exit, so it doesn't meet the review either. What was asked for is a different change from this one:

- a user-configurable backend setting that keeps `WGPU_BACKEND` and `FIELDCRAFT_GPU`;
- Vulkan by default on Linux and Windows and Metal on macOS;
- backends that aren't supported yet listed as "coming soon";
- a visible message, with a fallback where possible, when the chosen backend has no adapter.

That needs a rework, and a fix-up pass can't produce it. The rules, ADR and ROADMAP edits that record a "Vulkan only" decision also still need to come out. The branch also conflicts with develop, because `docs/lbm-collision.md` and `docs/lbm-gpu-performance.md` moved under `docs/manual/`.

The parts worth keeping in the rework are the explicit `webgpu`/`webgl` features for wasm, `wgpu_no_default_features` (so feature unification can't silently re-enable native backends), and the single process-wide instance in `select.rs`.

